Configuration file format for NatAmbio (natambio)
================================================================================

natambio is configured by a single XML file passed on the command line. Lines
that should be ignored can be wrapped in standard XML comments (<!-- ... -->).
Tag content with leading/trailing whitespace is taken as-is.

This document describes the schema as accepted by the parser in src/naconf.cpp.
Tags not listed here are silently ignored. Several example configurations live
under the docs/config_samples/ directory.


Document structure
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The parser scans for the first <natambio> element either as the root element
or as a direct child of the root element. Everything outside <natambio> is
ignored. A typical wrapper looks like:

    <main>
      <natambio>
        ... blocks below ...
      </natambio>
    </main>

Inside <natambio> the following top-level blocks are recognised, in any order:

    <coeff>        one convolution coefficient set (from a file or derived
                   from the convolution of other coeffs)
    <xtc>         a XTC crosstalk-cancellation filter generator that
                   synthesises a pair of coeffs (direct + cross)
    <xtc_asym>     the asymmetric variant of <xtc>, for layouts where the two
                   speakers sit at different azimuths; synthesises three coeffs
                   (a shared direct + one cross per speaker)
    <low_and_high_filter>
                   a crossover filter generator that synthesises a
                   complementary low-pass / high-pass coeff pair
    <loudness>     an equal-loudness compensation filter generator that
                   synthesises a single minimum-phase coeff
    <convol>       one convolution routing channel
    <nae>     one PCA spatial-decomposition engine
    <jack_input>   JACK client name and input ports
    <jack_output>  JACK output ports

<coeff>, <xtc>, <xtc_asym>, <low_and_high_filter>, <loudness>, <convol> and
<nae> may appear any number of times.
<jack_input> and <jack_output> should appear once each.


Sample rate
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
There is no global sample-rate tag. Before parsing the configuration, natambio
opens a minimal short-lived JACK client to read the running server's sample
rate, and uses it for everything:

  - the <xtc>, <xtc_asym>, <low_and_high_filter> and <loudness> blocks generate
    their filters at that rate (so they take no <sample_rate> tag), and
  - every <coeff> loaded from a <filename> is checked against it; a file whose
    sample rate differs aborts the program with an error.

JACK must therefore be running before natambio is started.


Gain convention
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All dB gain fields share one convention -- the `<gain>` fields (in <coeff>,
<convol> and <low_and_high_filter>) and the NAE gain fields (<front_gain>,
<ambience_gain>, <rear_gain>) are plain dB gains converted internally via
`pow(10, dB/20)`:

    positive value  -> amplification (louder)
    negative value  -> attenuation (quieter)
    0               -> unity

For the NAE gain fields, omitting the value for the active mode is a parse
error (an unset gain cannot be told apart from 0 dB internally).


<coeff> -- convolution coefficient set
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A coeff holds one set of convolution coefficients. It is obtained in one of two
ways:

  (a) loaded from an audio file (<filename>), or
  (b) derived from the convolution of other coeffs (one or more <convol_coeff>).

All <coeff> blocks must appear before any <convol> that references them, and a
derived coeff must appear after all the coeffs it references (convol_coeffs are
resolved in document order, after the whole file has been read).

(a) File-loaded coeff
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Loads one channel from an audio file (any libsndfile-supported format).

    <coeff>
      <name>direct left</name>
      <filename>~/IR/direct_left.wav</filename>
      <channel>1</channel>
      <skip>0</skip>
      <length>8192</length>
      <gain>0.0</gain>
    </coeff>

    name         Unique identifier referenced by <convol>/<coeff_name>.       (required)
    filename     Path to the audio file. `~` expands to $HOME.                 (required unless
                                                                                <convol_coeff> is used)
    channel      1-based channel index to read.                                (optional, default 1)
    skip         Frames to skip at the start of the file.                      (optional, default 0)
    length       Frames to read. When omitted (or 0) the whole file is read    (optional, default:
                 and length is set to the file's total frame count, or that     whole file)
                 count minus <skip> when leading frames are skipped.
    gain         Per-coefficient gain in dB (+ amplifies, - attenuates).        (optional, default 0)

The file's sample rate must equal the JACK sample rate. natambio probes the
running JACK server's sample rate at start-up (before parsing) and checks every
loaded file against it; a mismatch aborts with an error.

(b) Derived (convolved) coeff
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
When one or more <convol_coeff> tags are present, <filename> is ignored and the
coeff is built, after the whole config is parsed, by convolving the referenced
coeffs together (left to right):

    <coeff>
      <name>XTC EQ direct left</name>
      <convol_coeff>XTC direct</convol_coeff>   <!-- convolved with... -->
      <convol_coeff>DRC EQ left</convol_coeff>   <!-- ...this one        -->
      <length>16384</length>
    </coeff>

    name         Unique identifier referenced by <convol>/<coeff_name>.       (required)
    convol_coeff Name of another <coeff> to convolve. Repeatable (1..n).      (>= 1)
    length       Output length in samples. The convolution result is          (optional, default:
                 truncated (or zero-padded) to this length; 0/omitted keeps    full convolution
                 the full linear-convolution length.                           length)
    gain         Extra gain in dB applied to the result                       (optional, default 0)
                 (+ amplifies, - attenuates).

With a single <convol_coeff> the referenced coeff is copied (truncated/padded to
<length>); with several they are convolved in sequence. The convolution is a
single-precision FFT convolution (the fft_convolve_truncate routine in src/dsp.c).

When <length> is omitted the derived coeff is set to the full linear-convolution
length, i.e. the sum of the referenced coeffs' lengths minus (count - 1). This
is computed while parsing when every referenced coeff is already declared;
references to coeffs generated later (<xtc>, <xtc_asym>, <low_and_high_filter>,
<loudness>)
are instead resolved when the convolutions are built.

All referenced coeffs must share the same sample rate; the derived coeff
inherits it. As with file-loaded coeffs, that sample rate must equal the JACK
sample rate.

Referenced coeffs may themselves be derived coeffs, as long as they are declared
earlier in the file.

Special name: a <convol> that references `<coeff_name>delta</coeff_name>`
activates bypass mode (no convolution; only the convol's delay and
gain are applied to the mixed input).


<xtc> -- XTC crosstalk-cancellation filter generator
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A <xtc> block synthesises, from scratch, a pair of FIR filters implementing
the XTC (crosstalk cancellation) recursion combined with
a log-empirical ILD model fitted to public HRTF datasets (HUTUBS, RIEC, CIPIC,
BiLi, ARI). It produces two coeffs -- a direct-path filter and a
cross-path filter -- that are added to the coeff list under the names given by
<direct_filter_name> / <cross_filter_name>, exactly as if they had been loaded
from a file. They can then be referenced by <convol>/<coeff_name> (see the
four <convol> blocks in docs/config_samples/convol_drc_xtc.xml) or even by a derived
coeff's <convol_coeff>.

    <xtc>
      <itd_us>180</itd_us>
      <ild_db>10</ild_db>
      <ild_alpha>1.8</ild_alpha>
      <azimuth_deg>20</azimuth_deg>
      <length>4096</length>
      <direct_filter_name>XTC direct</direct_filter_name>
      <cross_filter_name>XTC cross</cross_filter_name>
    </xtc>

    itd_us              Inter-aural time difference, microseconds. Converted   (required)
                        to the XTC recursion delay using the JACK sample rate.
    ild_db              Inter-aural level difference per recursion step, in dB  (required)
                        of attenuation.
    ild_alpha           Log-empirical ILD model scale factor.                  (required)
    azimuth_deg         Source azimuth, degrees, fed to the ILD model.         (required)
    length              Length of each generated filter, in samples.           (required, > 0)
    direct_filter_name  Name of the resulting direct-path coeff.               (required)
    cross_filter_name   Name of the resulting cross-path coeff.                (required)

All <xtc> parameters are mandatory; omitting any one is a parse error.

The two generated coeffs are created at the JACK sample rate (see "Sample rate"
above; there is no <sample_rate> tag). The filter names must not collide with
any other coeff name. XTC coeffs are generated after all <coeff> blocks have
been read, so a derived coeff may convolve them via <convol_coeff>.


<xtc_asym> -- asymmetric XTC filter generator
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Same generator as <xtc>, for rooms where the two speakers are NOT at the same
azimuth from the listening position. The geometry is given twice, one <left>
and one <right> sub-block, and the block produces THREE coeffs: a direct-path
filter shared by both channels plus one cross-path filter per speaker.

The direct filter is shared because it depends only on the product of the two
crossed paths, so the asymmetry cancels out of it; what differs between the two
channels is the cross filter. Each cross filter carries the OTHER speaker's
ITD, since it is the one cancelling that speaker's crosstalk: cross_left, which
feeds the left speaker, leads with the right side's delay.

    <xtc_asym>
      <left>
        <itd_us>180</itd_us>
        <ild_db>10</ild_db>
        <ild_alpha>1.8</ild_alpha>
        <azimuth_deg>20</azimuth_deg>
      </left>
      <right>
        <itd_us>140</itd_us>
        <ild_db>8</ild_db>
        <ild_alpha>1.8</ild_alpha>
        <azimuth_deg>15</azimuth_deg>
      </right>
      <length>4096</length>
      <direct_filter_name>XTC direct</direct_filter_name>
      <cross_left_filter_name>XTC cross L</cross_left_filter_name>
      <cross_right_filter_name>XTC cross R</cross_right_filter_name>
    </xtc_asym>

    left, right             One sub-block per speaker, each holding that   (both required)
                            side's itd_us, ild_db, ild_alpha and
                            azimuth_deg, with the same meaning and the
                            same units as in <xtc>. All four are
                            required in each sub-block.
    length                  Length of each generated filter, in samples.   (required, > 0)
                            Common to all three.
    direct_filter_name      Name of the resulting direct-path coeff,       (required)
                            shared by both channels.
    cross_left_filter_name  Name of the cross coeff feeding the LEFT       (required)
                            speaker.
    cross_right_filter_name Name of the cross coeff feeding the RIGHT      (required)
                            speaker.

All <xtc_asym> parameters are mandatory; omitting any one is a parse error.

The routing is identical to the symmetric case -- the same four <convol> paths,
with two distinct cross coeff names instead of one repeated. A complete example
is in docs/config_samples/convol_drc_xtc_asym.xml.

Balance
.......
<xtc_asym> has no balance parameter. If the two channels do not arrive at the
same level at the listening position, trim them with the <gain> of the TWO
<convol> blocks that share the affected <to_output> -- both the direct and the
cross one, regardless of which input each takes.

Always ATTENUATE the channel that arrives louder (a negative <gain>) and leave
the other at 0 dB. The strict correction is a boost on the quieter channel, but
only the ratio between the two matters and attenuating spends no headroom.

To set it, play a mono signal through both channels and attenuate until the
image sits centred; that is accurate enough. Aim to get within about 1 dB,
because the balance is part of the cancellation and not a cosmetic trim: a
residual balance error b caps the achievable crosstalk cancellation at roughly
20*log10|1-b|.

    balance error       cancellation ceiling
    0.5 dB              about -25 dB
    1   dB              about -19 dB
    2   dB              about -14 dB
    3   dB              about -11 dB
    6   dB              about  -6 dB

If the DRC stage already levels both channels against a common target, the
balance is already handled and both gains stay at 0 dB.

An asymmetric layout is not expected to reach the performance of an equivalent
symmetric one; this block exists to make a compromised room usable. The model
is developed in docs/xtc/xtc_no_simetrico_es.md.


<low_and_high_filter> -- complementary crossover filter generator
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A <low_and_high_filter> block synthesises, from scratch, a complementary pair of
FIR filters that split a signal at a crossover frequency: a low-pass and a
high-pass. It produces two coeffs added to the coeff list under the names given
by <low_pass_coeff_name> / <high_pass_coeff_name>, exactly as if they had been
loaded from a file. They can then be referenced by <convol>/<coeff_name> (see
the four <convol> blocks in docs/config_samples/sub_monitors_convol_dipole.xml --
a typical subwoofer / satellite split) or by a derived coeff's <convol_coeff>.

    <low_and_high_filter>
      <frequency>100.0</frequency>
      <dB_octave>24</dB_octave>
      <gain>0</gain>
      <length>8192</length>
      <low_pass_coeff_name>Low pass FIR</low_pass_coeff_name>
      <high_pass_coeff_name>High pass FIR</high_pass_coeff_name>
    </low_and_high_filter>

    frequency             Crossover (cut-off) frequency, Hz.                 (required, > 0)
    dB_octave             Low-pass roll-off slope above the crossover, in    (required)
                          dB per octave.
    gain                  Pass-band gain applied to both filters, dB         (optional, default 0)
                          (+ amplifies, - attenuates).
    length                Length of each generated filter, in samples.       (required, > 0)
    low_pass_coeff_name   Name of the resulting low-pass coeff.              (required)
    high_pass_coeff_name  Name of the resulting high-pass coeff.             (required)

All <low_and_high_filter> parameters are mandatory except <gain> (optional,
default 0 dB); omitting any other is a parse error.

The low-pass is designed with firwin2 (a flat pass-band at <gain> dB up to
<frequency>, then a constant <dB_octave> dB/octave roll-off). The high-pass is
its exact complement -- a gain-adjusted linear-phase delta minus the
low-pass -- so that the two filters sum back to a gain-adjusted delta. Both are
then converted to minimum phase, and those minimum-phase filters are what get
applied.

The two generated coeffs are created at the JACK sample rate (see "Sample rate"
above; there is no <sample_rate> tag). The filter names must not collide with
any other coeff name. They are generated after all <coeff> blocks have been
read, so a derived coeff may convolve them via <convol_coeff>.


<loudness> -- equal-loudness compensation filter generator
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A <loudness> block synthesises a single FIR filter that follows an equal-loudness
(isophonic) contour. It produces one coeff -- added to the coeff list under the
name given by <filter_name> -- whose magnitude is the DIFFERENCE between the
chosen model's contour at <phon> and its contour at <ref_phon> (each contour
normalised to 0 dB at 1 kHz). The result is a "playback at <phon>, mastered for
<ref_phon>" loudness compensation curve: it is flat (0 dB) at 1 kHz and boosts
the low (and high) frequencies when <phon> is below <ref_phon>. The coeff is
minimum phase.

    <loudness>
      <phon>70</phon>
      <ref_phon>90</ref_phon>
      <model>fletcher-munson</model>
      <length>4096</length>
      <filter_name>loudness filter</filter_name>
    </loudness>

    phon         Target listening level, phon.                              (required)
    ref_phon     Reference (mastering) level, phon; subtracted from the     (required)
                 target contour.
    model        Equal-loudness model. One of:                              (required)
                   iso226-2003, iso226-2023  (analytic ISO 226 formula)
                   fletcher-munson           (tabulated 1933 curves)
                   a-weighting, b-weighting, c-weighting
                                             (IEC weightings; these ignore
                                              phon, so the difference curve is
                                              flat and the filter is a unit
                                              impulse)
                 (robinson-dadson is not implemented.)
    length       Length of the generated filter, in samples.                (required, > 0)
    filter_name  Name of the resulting coeff.                               (required)

The contour difference is sampled by firwin2 (a flat-at-1 kHz target curve,
log-interpolated/extrapolated from the model's tabulated points, rolling off to
silence outside 10 Hz - 20 kHz) and the resulting linear-phase filter is
converted to minimum phase. The generated coeff is created at the JACK sample
rate (see "Sample rate" above; there is no <sample_rate> tag). Its name must not
collide with any other coeff name. It is generated after all <coeff> blocks have
been read, so a derived coeff may convolve it via <convol_coeff> (see
docs/config_samples/nae_dipole_loudness_drc.xml, which convolves it with a DRC EQ
filter).


<jack_input> -- JACK client name and input ports
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    <jack_input>
      <clientname>natambio</clientname>
      <port>
        <name>input_left</name>
        <destname>system:capture_1</destname>
      </port>
      <port>
        <name>input_right</name>
        <destname>system:capture_2</destname>
      </port>
    </jack_input>

    clientname   JACK client name. Default: "natambio".                        (optional, one tag)
    port         JACK input port definition. May appear multiple times.        (>= 1 required)
      name       Port name (as seen on the JACK graph).                        (required)
      destname   External port to auto-connect on startup.                     (optional)


<jack_output> -- JACK output ports
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    <jack_output>
      <port>
        <name>output_left</name>
        <destname>system:playback_1</destname>
      </port>
      ...
    </jack_output>

    port         JACK output port definition. May appear multiple times.       (>= 1 required)
      name       Port name.                                                    (required)
      destname   External port to auto-connect on startup.                     (optional)


<nae> -- PCA spatial decomposition
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Each <nae> block defines ONE NAE engine. Use multiple <nae>
blocks for multiple engines.

    <nae>
      <name>front stereo</name>
      <mode>alpha</mode>
      <steps_length>5</steps_length>
      <front_gain>-2.0</front_gain>
      <ambience_gain>2.0</ambience_gain>
      <input_left>input_left</input_left>
      <input_right>input_right</input_right>
      <output_left>pn_output_left</output_left>
      <output_right>pn_output_right</output_right>
    </nae>

    name             Label for diagnostics.                                    (optional)
    mode             "alpha" (front dipole) or "beta" (rear dipole).           (required)
    steps_length     PCA analysis window length, in JACK buffer periods        (optional,
                     (the covariance spans steps_length blocks).                default 5)
    front_gain       Main-component gain, dB (positive = amplification;        (required for alpha)
                     see Gain convention). (alpha mode only)
    ambience_gain    Ambience-component gain, dB (positive = amplification).   (required for alpha)
                     (alpha mode only)
    rear_gain        Ambient-component gain, dB (positive = amplification).     (required for beta)
                     (beta mode only)
    pan_scale        Mid/side rescale of both components, [-1, 1].            (optional,
                     Each component is held in a mid and a side coordinate,     default 0)
                     and the control grows one against the other before they
                     are turned into left and right: mid *= (1 + pan_scale),
                     side *= (1 - pan_scale). A POSITIVE value closes towards
                     mono and a negative one opens towards the sides. Because
                     both coordinates move, the balance between direct sound
                     and ambience travels with the setting. In beta mode, which
                     emits the ambient component alone, only that component is
                     rescaled. See the notes below.
    pan_rotate       Rotation of the placement frame, [-1, 1].                 (optional,
                     Turns the pair of axes the two components are placed on,   default 0)
                     as a rigid pair, without touching the projections: what is
                     extracted does not change, only where it is put. The angle
                     the principal component ends up at is a fraction of the
                     one the decomposition measured, so +1 lands it exactly on
                     the mid axis and the ambient one on the side axis -- a
                     clean mono / anti-phase pair -- while -1 exchanges their
                     roles. Being an orthonormal rotation it leaves the level
                     of each component untouched, so unlike pan_scale it does
                     not move the direct/ambience balance. See the notes below.
    pan_scale_corr   How much of pan_scale follows the input correlation,      (optional,
                     [0, 1]. At 0 pan_scale is the fixed factor it always        default 0)
                     was; at 1 the factor applied is pan_scale times the
                     correlation, so a correlated passage takes the full
                     setting and a decorrelated one is left alone. The reading
                     is the decomposition's own: on balanced material
                     (eig0 - eig1)/(eig0 + eig1) is exactly the inter-channel
                     correlation, so nothing extra is measured. See the notes.
    pan_scale_tau    Time constant of the placement smoothing, seconds.        (optional,
                     Applies to both controls above; ignored without them.       default 0.5)
    input_left       Source JACK input port for the left channel.              (required)
    input_right      Source JACK input port for the right channel.             (required)
    output_left      Combined output (alpha: main + ambience; beta: the       (required, or
                     ambient component), written in BOTH modes: a          front/amb equivalent)
                     <jack_output>/<port>/<name>, or a virtual name
                     consumed by <convol>/<from_nae>.
    output_right     Right output (same resolution as output_left).            (required, or
                                                                                front/amb equivalent)
    front_output_left, front_output_right
                     Optional separate outputs for the main (front)
                     component on its own (alpha mode).                        (optional)
    amb_output_left, amb_output_right
                     Optional separate outputs for the ambience component
                     on its own (in beta mode they carry the ambient
                     signal); same name resolution as output_left/right.       (optional)

pan_scale and pan_rotate, what to expect:

    They are different operations, not two spellings of one. pan_scale changes
    the RATIO between the two coordinates, which moves the panning and, because
    the level of each component follows the two factors, the balance between
    direct sound and ambience with it: on test material that balance travels
    about 19 dB across the range. pan_rotate turns the frame rigidly, which
    moves the panning while leaving both levels exactly where they were,
    measured at 0.00 dB across the whole range. Reach for the first to trade
    direct against ambience, for the second to place them without touching the
    mix. Both take positive towards mono and negative towards the sides.

    pan_rotate tracks the programme, and that is what it is for. The angle is a
    fraction of the one the decomposition measures block by block, so at +1 the
    principal component stays locked on mono however the material wanders --
    measured on a moving source whose axis swings between 2 and 41 degrees, it
    holds at 0.00 dB with no drift at all, where a fixed rotation of the same
    average angle still drifts 4.10 dB. No matrix downstream of the engine can
    do that, since it would have to know where the axis is.

    Each component flips its quiet channel wholesale, and the two do it on
    opposite sides of pan_scale. A component's two channels carry the same
    waveform at different levels, so the quieter one does not invert sample by
    sample: it inverts entirely, and past the flip it grows again in its new
    polarity. It is held there to half the level of the other, which bounds the
    setting symmetrically at |pan_scale| <= (3 - t)/(3 + t) for t the tangent of
    the placement angle: 0.8 on material whose axis sits at 18 degrees.

    pan_rotate is not bounded, and its negative half has an unstable middle.
    Both ends are well defined pure states and the rotation preserves energy
    exactly, so there is nothing to clamp; but on the way to the side axis the
    frame passes 45 degrees, where one channel of each component vanishes and
    the panning is momentarily unbounded. On material whose axis sits at 18
    degrees that transit spans roughly -0.15 to -0.55, with the image wandering
    up to 12 dB against 3.8 dB untouched, and it settles again by -0.7. Where
    it falls depends on the material, at (theta - 45)/(90 - theta). The positive
    half has no such region, running monotonically from the measured angle down
    to zero.

    pan_scale_corr makes the control breathe with the music. It tightens
    during a correlated passage and releases over a decorrelated one -- a
    reverb tail, a wide pad -- so with pan_scale positive it sharpens what is
    already sharp and leaves the diffuse alone, widening the contrast between
    direct sound and ambience; with pan_scale negative it does the reverse and
    flattens that contrast. Two things to keep in mind. The reading cannot tell
    a correlation of +0.7 from one of -0.7, and on anti-correlated material the
    decomposition has already swapped which component is the direct one, so
    that region behaves in a way the setting does not describe. And a level
    imbalance between channels reads as decorrelation: with the right channel
    8 dB down, material at 0.60 measures 0.83, because part of the side
    variance is panning rather than decorrelation.

    The placement is smoothed, and the time constant is worth tuning. The axis
    wanders from block to block and these controls multiply the imbalance, so
    they multiply that wander too. The axis used to place the components is a
    one pole average of its own rotation. On moving material the worst jump
    between windows falls from 1.65 dB to 1.55 at 0.5 s and to 0.86 at 2 s;
    long values also smooth the genuine movement of the programme, though, so
    around 0.5 s keeps the jumpiness down without the image stopping following
    the music.

Mode semantics:
    alpha (front dipole): writes main component to *output_left/right channels,
           ambience is mixed in via the ambience_gain.
    beta (rear dipole) : writes the ambient signal to *output_left/right channels
           using inter-channel correlation to distinguish centred from
           decorrelated content.

Port name resolution: when <output_left> / <output_right> / front_* / amb_*
match a <jack_output>/<port>/<name>, the signal is written directly to that
JACK output port. Otherwise the name acts as a virtual buffer that <convol>
blocks can pick up via <from_nae>.

See docs/config_samples/nae_alpha_beta_out.xml for a full example: a front engine
routed through its front/ambience outputs and a rear engine through plain
left/right outputs.


<convol> -- convolution routing channel
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Each <convol> defines one channel through the convolution engine. Inputs from
multiple sources are summed before convolution; outputs may fan out to several
sinks. There may be up to 64 <convol> blocks (zita-convolver hard limit).

    <convol>
      <name>direct left</name>
      <coeff_name>direct left</coeff_name>
      <from_input>input_left</from_input>
      <from_nae>pn_output_left</from_nae>
      <from_convol>some other convolver</from_convol>
      <to_output>output_left</to_output>
      <gain>-5.0</gain>
      <delay>0</delay>
    </convol>

    name           Unique identifier; targeted by other convolvers'           (required)
                   <from_convol>.
    coeff_name     Reference to a <coeff>/<name>, or "delta" for bypass.      (required)
    from_input     Mix in a <jack_input>/<port>/<name>. Repeatable.           (>= 0)
    from_nae  Mix in a NAE virtual output (left/right/front/amb).   (>= 0)
                   Repeatable.
    from_convol    Mix in another <convol>'s output by name. Repeatable.      (>= 0)
    to_output      Send convolver output to a <jack_output>/<port>/<name>.    (>= 0)
                   Repeatable. A convol with no <to_output> is intermediate-
                   only and feeds other convolvers via <from_convol>.
    gain           Output gain in dB (+ amplifies, - attenuates).             (optional, default 0)
    delay          Output delay in samples (sample-accurate ring buffer).      (optional, default 0)

Care must be taken not to create convolution loops with chained <from_convol>
references.


Example configurations
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ready-to-use configurations for the current schema live under
docs/config_samples/, each illustrating one feature or a full system (the
file-loaded ones reference IR WAVs you must provide). See
docs/config_samples/README.md for the annotated catalogue, organised by use
case: convol-only stereo, NAE patch-panel (no convolution), stereo + NAE front
dipole, 2.1 with subwoofer crossover, split two-client chains, multiple parallel
output paths, A/B switching, and full two-dipole systems.


Notes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 - All example files under docs/config_samples/ now use the current schema.
   The schema above reflects what the parser in src/naconf.cpp accepts; tags it
   does not recognise are silently ignored, which can mask configuration errors
   (so watch for typos in tag names).
 - An extended developer-oriented description of the program architecture
   (not the file format) lives in src/README.md.
